`),parts:[{question:"To what extent is human resource management effective in this business?",sampleAnswer:["Human resource management appears only moderately effective in this business.","The data shows some positive signs. Strike days fell from 12 to 3 and staff turnover fell from 18% to 12%, so there may be fewer disputes and better retention of employees.","However, other indicators suggest important problems remain. Absenteeism rose from 4% to 6% and accidents increased from 8 to 11, which points to lower morale, weaker supervision, poor training, or unsafe work practices.","Training expenditure per employee also fell from $1,200 to $700. This may have reduced costs in the short term, but it can also leave employees less prepared and may help explain the increase in accidents and absenteeism.","Overall, the business has improved in some areas of industrial relations, but the worsening safety and absence data means human resource management cannot be judged fully effective."].join(`
`),marks:4},{question:"Using the data, analyse the interdependence between human resource management and profitability management in this business.",sampleAnswer:["Human resource management and profitability management are interdependent because decisions about employees affect both costs and revenue.","The fall in training expenditure from $1,200 to $700 per employee may have helped reduce fixed costs from $350,000 to $320,000 and improve the expense ratio from 42% to 38%. In the short term, that looks positive for profitability.","However, the same period also shows absenteeism rising from 4% to 6% and accidents increasing from 8 to 11. These outcomes can reduce productivity, increase downtime, create workers compensation costs, and lower service quality.","That means a financial improvement caused by cutting staff-related spending may be temporary if poor human resource outcomes later increase hidden costs or reduce output.","The relationship also works the other way. If the business focuses too heavily on profit targets, it may underinvest in training and safety. If it invests more effectively in staff, profitability may strengthen over time through better retention, fewer accidents, and higher productivity.","Therefore, the data shows that managing employees well is not separate from profitability management. Each function directly influences the performance of the other."].join(`
`),marks:6}]})}},foe=["BS-MK-2","BS-HR-4"],goe=["operations strategies and performance objectives","human resource strategies and employment relations","marketing strategies and the marketing process","legal and ethical responsibilities in business"],$oe={id:"business_studies/2015/q26",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...foe],learningTopics:goe,generate(){return Ee({question:["Write a business report for Fit Fast Bikes.","","The business sells exercise bicycles and promises unrealistic weight-loss results. Sales staff are paid commission only, promise refunds to customers, but the business refuses repairs or refunds and many products are poor quality.","","In your report:","- describe consumer laws the business may be breaking","- propose human resource strategies to manage the current sales staff more effectively","- explain how marketing and human resources are interdependent in this business"].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["Fit Fast Bikes is exposed to serious legal, marketing, and human resource problems. The business is not just selling exercise bikes poorly; it is creating customer expectations that it cannot lawfully or ethically meet. To improve performance, it must change both what it promises to customers and how staff are managed.","The business may be breaching consumer law in several ways. Promising unrealistic weight-loss results can be misleading or deceptive conduct because the advertising overstates what the product can do. Selling poor-quality bikes while refusing repairs or refunds may also breach consumer guarantees, because products must be of acceptable quality and businesses cannot avoid their obligations simply by refusing complaints. Promising refunds through sales staff and then refusing to provide them creates another legal risk because customers may have been misled at the point of sale.","Human resource strategies should focus on changing the behaviour of the sales team. Training is needed so staff understand consumer law, ethical selling, and the product features they are allowed to claim. Performance management should monitor whether staff are meeting legal and customer-service standards, not just sales targets. The reward system should also change. A commission-only structure encourages aggressive selling and can push employees to say whatever is needed to close a sale. A mix of wages and performance incentives linked to customer satisfaction, low complaint levels, and compliance would be more effective.","Marketing and human resources are interdependent in this business because employees are the people delivering the marketing promise. If marketing claims are unrealistic, staff may feel pressure to repeat them. If staff are poorly trained or rewarded only for sales volume, complaints and refund disputes are likely to rise. That damages the brand and makes future marketing less effective. On the other hand, better-trained and better-managed staff can support truthful promotion, stronger after-sales service, and improved customer trust.","Overall, Fit Fast Bikes needs to align its marketing claims, product quality, and staff behaviour. If the business fixes only one area, the same complaints and legal risks will continue. A coordinated response is therefore necessary."].join(`
`),marks:20})}},yoe=["BS-OP-5"],boe=["operations strategies and performance objectives","marketing strategies and the marketing process","financial objectives, ratios, and financial strategies"],xoe={id:"business_studies/2015/q27",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...yoe],learningTopics:boe,generate(){return Ee({question:"Evaluate the importance of operations strategies in achieving competitive advantage.",sampleAnswer:["Operations strategies are very important in achieving competitive advantage because they shape the cost, quality, speed, dependability, and flexibility of the final product or service. These are the areas customers notice directly, even if they do not see the operations system behind them.","For example, quality management can give a business an advantage by reducing defects and building a stronger reputation. Technology can increase productivity and lower unit costs, which helps a business compete on price or improve profit margins. Supply chain management and inventory management can also improve dependability by ensuring the right inputs are available at the right time.","Operations strategies can also support differentiation. A business that customises products effectively, maintains consistent quality, or delivers quickly may stand out from competitors even if its price is not the lowest. In this way, operations strategies contribute to both cost leadership and product differentiation.","However, operations strategies are not automatically successful. New technology can be expensive, outsourcing can reduce quality control, and poor inventory decisions can tie up cash or create shortages. This means the importance of any strategy depends on how well it matches the business objectives and the nature of the market.","Overall, operations strategies are highly important because they turn business objectives into day-to-day performance. A business may have strong marketing or finance, but if operations cannot deliver value to customers efficiently and reliably, competitive advantage will be difficult to sustain."].join(`
