`),marks:3}]})}},Fae=["BS-OP-5"],Dae=["operations strategies and performance objectives","human resource strategies and employment relations","marketing strategies and the marketing process","financial objectives, ratios, and financial strategies"],zae={id:"business_studies/2016/q25",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Fae],learningTopics:Dae,generate(){return Ee({question:"With reference to a business you have studied, analyse the relationship between cost and quality in the operations of the business.",sampleAnswer:["Cost and quality are closely related because operations decisions usually involve a trade-off between spending more now and achieving better outcomes later. In many businesses, higher quality requires better raw materials, more skilled labour, stricter quality control, or newer technology, all of which can raise operating costs in the short term.","However, the relationship is not simply that quality always increases cost. Better quality can reduce waste, rework, returns, and customer complaints. That means a business may spend more to prevent problems, but save money later through more efficient operations and stronger customer loyalty.","For a business studied in class, this relationship should be shown through specific operations strategies. For example, a business that invests in quality assurance or high-grade inputs may initially face higher production costs, but it may also achieve a stronger reputation and repeat sales. That can improve long-term profitability.","The relationship can also become negative if managers cut costs too aggressively. Cheaper suppliers, lower training, or reduced maintenance may lower costs in the short term but damage quality, leading to customer dissatisfaction and higher hidden costs later.","Therefore, the relationship between cost and quality is interdependent rather than opposite. Effective operations management tries to find the point where quality is high enough to meet customer expectations while costs are controlled well enough to remain competitive."].join(`
