`),marks:20})}},l0e=["BS-OP-3","BS-OP-5"],h0e=["operations strategies and performance objectives","human resource strategies and employment relations","marketing strategies and the marketing process","financial objectives, ratios, and financial strategies"],d0e={id:"business_studies/2024/q27",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...l0e],learningTopics:h0e,generate(){return Ee({question:"How can operations strategies affect corporate social responsibility?",sampleAnswer:["Operations strategies affect corporate social responsibility because many ethical and social outcomes are created by the way the business produces and delivers its goods or services.","Sourcing decisions influence whether suppliers treat workers fairly and meet environmental standards. Production decisions affect waste, energy use, and safety. Quality management affects whether customers receive reliable products, while supply chain decisions affect how responsibly the business behaves across all stages of production.","Operations strategies can improve CSR when they reduce waste, protect employees, ensure safe products, and go beyond minimum legal compliance. For example, better inventory management can reduce spoilage and waste, while careful supplier selection can improve ethical standards.","However, operations strategies can also damage CSR if the business focuses only on cost. Outsourcing to low-cost suppliers without checking labour standards, or cutting corners on quality and safety, may improve short-term efficiency but weaken social responsibility.","Overall, operations strategies affect CSR significantly because responsible behaviour is often decided through everyday operational choices, not just through public statements."].join(`
