`),sampleAnswer:["XYZ Equipment Pty Ltd wants to relocate overseas because Australian production costs are high and opportunities for growth exist in Asia. This decision affects staffing, operations planning, and the global strategies the business uses to achieve cost leadership.","A relevant human resource management process is separation, because closing the Australian factory is likely to make some roles redundant. Separation must be managed carefully through communication, legal compliance, possible redundancy payments, and support for affected employees. Acquisition may then become important in the new location as the business recruits workers with the skills needed for the overseas factory.","The relocation chart is important because it shows the sequence and timing of tasks such as closing the old factory, sourcing suppliers, building the new factory, and arranging warehousing. If the order is poor or the timing is unrealistic, delays can occur and some tasks may be left waiting for others to be completed first. That increases cost and risk during the relocation.","Several global factors can help the business achieve cost leadership. Global sourcing may reduce input costs by giving access to cheaper suppliers. Economies of scale may lower unit costs if the new factory produces at larger volume. Scanning and learning can help the business adopt better overseas production methods, while research and development can improve efficiency and product design over time.","Overall, the relocation should not be seen as just a move in location. It is a major strategic change that requires coordinated human resource management, careful planning, and global strategies that genuinely reduce cost without creating new operational problems."].join(`
`),marks:20})}},zye=["BS-MK-3","BS-MK-4"],Yye=["operations strategies and performance objectives","marketing strategies and the marketing process"],Wye={id:"business_studies/2025/q26",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...zye],learningTopics:Yye,generate(){return Ee({question:"Explain how target markets affect e-marketing, people, processes and physical evidence.",sampleAnswer:["Target markets affect these strategies because a business should design its marketing and service delivery around the needs of the customers it wants to attract.","E-marketing must match the media habits and preferences of the target market. A younger market may respond well to social media and interactive online content, while another market may prefer more informative websites or email communication. The tone, message, and platform should all suit the chosen segment.","People are affected because staff need the right service style and communication skills for the target market. Processes are also affected because the way the service is delivered should fit customer expectations about convenience, speed, or personal attention. Physical evidence matters because visible cues such as store layout, packaging, branding, and website design all influence whether the target market sees the offering as suitable and trustworthy.","These areas are linked. If the target market is clear, the business can design consistent strategies across digital marketing, staff behaviour, service delivery, and the visible image of the business.","Overall, target markets affect all four strategy areas because each one must be aligned with what the chosen customers value and expect."].join(`
`),marks:20})}},Gye=["BS-FN-1","BS-FN-4"],Uye=["human resource strategies and employment relations","financial objectives, ratios, and financial strategies"],Vye={id:"business_studies/2025/q27",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Gye],learningTopics:Uye,generate(){return Ee({question:"Explain how financial strategies can achieve liquidity and profitability objectives.",sampleAnswer:["Financial strategies can achieve liquidity by helping the business maintain enough cash to meet short-term obligations, and they can achieve profitability by increasing revenue or controlling costs.","For liquidity, strategies such as discounts for early payment and factoring can improve the speed at which cash enters the business. Leasing or sale and lease back can also improve cash flow by reducing the amount of money tied up in assets. These strategies help the business pay suppliers, employees, and other short-term debts on time.","For profitability, the business may use expense controls, budgeting, and better management of working capital to reduce unnecessary costs. Strong profitability management can also involve pricing, cost control, and careful use of finance so returns are improved over time.","Some strategies affect both objectives at once. For example, tighter receivables control may improve liquidity immediately and reduce bad debts, which also supports profit. However, there can be trade-offs. A strategy that improves cash quickly may create extra costs, so managers need to balance short-term liquidity with long-term profitability.","Overall, financial strategies help achieve both objectives when they are chosen carefully and managed together rather than in isolation."].join(`
