`),marks:2},{question:["Using collision theory, explain the change in the concentration of CO after time $T$."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["At time $T$, the concentration of CO is decreased suddenly.","","A decrease in the concentration of CO results in a decrease in the **rate of the forward reaction** because there are fewer CO molecules per unit volume, leading to fewer collisions between CO and $\\mathrm{H_2O}$ molecules per unit time.","","The rate of the reverse reaction is now greater than the rate of the forward reaction, so the concentrations of $\\mathrm{CO_2(g)}$ and $\\mathrm{H_2(g)}$ decrease while $\\mathrm{CO(g)}$ and $\\mathrm{H_2O(g)}$ increase.","","As [CO] increases, the rate of the forward reaction gradually increases until the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction, and a new equilibrium is established."].join(`
