`),marks:5},{question:["Explain why a chemist should use more than one spectroscopic technique to identify an organic compound. Use TWO spectroscopic techniques to support your answer."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["Different spectroscopic techniques provide different types of structural information. No single technique gives a complete picture of a molecule's structure, so multiple techniques are used to confirm an identification.","","For example, **mass spectrometry** provides the molecular mass (from the molecular ion peak) and information about structural fragments, but does not directly reveal the positions of atoms.","","**$^1\\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy** provides information about the number of distinct hydrogen environments and the relative number of hydrogen nuclei in each, allowing identification of functional groups and distinguishing between structural isomers.","","Together, these techniques complement each other and allow a more confident and complete identification."].join(`
