`),sampleAnswer:["The boiling points of all three compounds are determined by their **intermolecular forces**.","","Methanol and propanoic acid both possess $\\mathrm{O{-}H}$ groups and can form **hydrogen bonds** between molecules. However, methyl propanoate (the ester) cannot form hydrogen bonds (it has no $\\mathrm{O{-}H}$ or $\\mathrm{N{-}H}$ groups), only dipole–dipole and **dispersion forces**.","","One might expect the ester to have a lower boiling point than both. However, methyl propanoate has a significantly larger molecule than methanol and therefore has much stronger **dispersion forces** (which increase with the number of electrons).","","The combined effect of the greater dispersion forces in methyl propanoate outweighs the hydrogen bonding in the much smaller methanol molecule, giving methyl propanoate ($79.8^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$) a higher boiling point than methanol ($64.7^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$).","","This demonstrates that the ester does not always have the lowest boiling point."].join(`
