`),sampleAnswer:["**General trend:** For both acids, pH decreases as concentration increases — higher concentration means more $\\mathrm{H^+}$ ions in solution.","","**Hydrochloric acid (strong acid):**","","$$\\mathrm{HCl(aq) \\rightarrow H^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq)}$$","","HCl fully dissociates at all concentrations. Therefore $[\\mathrm{H^+}] = [\\mathrm{HCl}]$ and the graph shows a **linear relationship** between pH and $\\log[\\mathrm{HCl}]$.","","**Acrylic acid (weak acid):**","","$$\\mathrm{C_2H_3COOH(aq) \\rightleftharpoons C_2H_3COO^-(aq) + H^+(aq)}$$","","$$K_\\mathrm{a} = \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{C_2H_3COO^-}][\\mathrm{H^+}]}{[\\mathrm{C_2H_3COOH}]}$$","","Acrylic acid only partially dissociates. The degree of dissociation increases as concentration decreases. This means that at higher concentrations, a **smaller fraction** dissociates, so $[\\mathrm{H^+}]$ does not increase proportionally — the line bends upward (higher pH than expected) at higher concentrations.","","The pH of acrylic acid is always **higher** than that of HCl at the same concentration because only a fraction of acrylic acid molecules provide $\\mathrm{H^+}$ ions."].join(`
