`),marks:2},{question:"What can be deduced about the system when the temperature is $T_1$, $T_2$ and $T_3$? Support your answer with reference to the graph.",sampleAnswer:["At all three temperatures, $\\Delta H$ is negative (exothermic reaction), and $T\\Delta S$ is negative (so $\\Delta S < 0$, entropy decreases).","","**At $T_1$:** $|\\Delta H| > |T\\Delta S|$, so $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0$. The reaction is **spontaneous** at this temperature.","","**At $T_2$:** The $\\Delta H$ and $T\\Delta S$ lines intersect, so $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S = 0$. The system is **at equilibrium** at this temperature.","","**At $T_3$:** $|T\\Delta S| > |\\Delta H|$, so $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S > 0$. The reaction is **non-spontaneous** at this temperature.","","As temperature increases, the increasingly negative $T\\Delta S$ term (due to negative $\\Delta S$) eventually overcomes the negative $\\Delta H$, making $\\Delta G$ positive."].join(`
