`),sampleAnswer:["**At $t_0$ (before HCl addition):**","Water has pH 7 (neutral, $[\\mathrm{H^+}] = 10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{mol\\,L^{-1}}$).","Solutions $X$ and $Y$ have pH 4.9 (acidic), indicating both contain a weak acid ($\\mathrm{HA}$) and its conjugate base ($\\mathrm{A^-}$) — they are **buffer solutions**.","","**At $t_1$ (HCl being added):**","Water's pH drops rapidly because $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is a strong acid that completely ionises, directly raising $[\\mathrm{H^+}]$.","","Solutions $X$ and $Y$ resist the change in pH — they act as **buffers**. The $\\mathrm{Cl^-}$ from HCl donates a proton to the conjugate base $\\mathrm{A^-}$:","$$\\mathrm{HCl(g) + A^-(aq) \\rightarrow HA(aq) + Cl^-(aq)}$$","This consumes $\\mathrm{H^+}$ before it can lower the pH significantly.","","**At $t_2$ (buffer capacity exhausted):**","Both $X$ and $Y$ start to decrease in pH as the $\\mathrm{A^-}$ is used up and can no longer neutralise incoming $\\mathrm{H^+}$.","$X$ decreases faster because it was a **less concentrated buffer** than $Y$, so its $\\mathrm{A^-}$ was exhausted more quickly."].join(`
