`),marks:2},{question:"Explain how TWO policy options could be implemented to reduce the NAIRU of this economy.",sampleAnswer:["One policy option is education and training. Governments can fund retraining, apprenticeships and vocational programmes so workers gain the skills needed in industries with job vacancies, reducing structural unemployment.","A second option is to improve labour mobility. Relocation assistance, better transport links and more efficient job-matching services help workers move to where jobs exist and reduce frictional unemployment.","By targeting structural and frictional unemployment, these policies lower the NAIRU over time."].join(`
`),marks:4}]}}},gtt=["EC-H4"],$tt=["objectives of monetary policy in Australia","how the RBA maintains the cash rate target","the transmission mechanism of lower interest rates"],ytt={id:"economics/2025/q24",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...gtt],learningTopics:$tt,generate(t){return{question:[],parts:[{question:"Outline the purpose of monetary policy in Australia.",sampleAnswer:["Monetary policy is used by the Reserve Bank of Australia to influence the cost and availability of credit in the economy.","Its purpose is to help stabilise economic activity and support objectives such as price stability, full employment and the economic welfare of Australians."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Explain how the Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) maintains the cash rate target.",sampleAnswer:["The RBA uses a policy interest rate corridor and its Exchange Settlement Account system to keep the market cash rate close to the target.","Because banks can borrow from the RBA at a rate above the target and deposit funds at a rate below the target, inter-bank rates tend to move toward the target rate.","The RBA can also use domestic market operations and reserve management to support that target."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:"Explain the impacts of a decrease in the cash rate on an economy.",sampleAnswer:["A decrease in the cash rate lowers borrowing costs and usually increases the availability of credit across the economy.","Households with loans have lower repayments and greater disposable income, so consumption tends to rise. Businesses also face cheaper finance, which encourages investment and expansion.","As consumption and investment increase, aggregate demand rises, lifting output and employment. Over time this can also support stronger economic growth.","However, if demand grows faster than productive capacity, inflationary pressures may emerge and the exchange rate may depreciate."].join(`
`),marks:5}]}}},btt=["EC-H2","EC-H4"],xtt=["balance of payments structure and trends","current account, primary income, and service exports","the savings-investment gap and capital flows"],wtt={id:"economics/2025/q25",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...btt],learningTopics:xtt,generate(t){return{question:["Explain the reasons for trends since 2016 in the size and composition of Australia's Balance of Payments. In your answer, refer to the information provided.","","The COVID-19 recession and associated international border closures resulted in a sharp decline in education and tourism service exports, but exports of these services have grown strongly in recent years following the re-opening of the border. The composition of Australia's capital flows has also changed significantly in the past decade or so. Unlike in the past, Australia's economy since 2017 has generated savings in excess of domestic investment.","",JBe()],sampleAnswer:["Since 2016, the size and composition of Australia's Balance of Payments have shifted toward stronger current account outcomes. The graph shows the current account balance moving from deficit toward surplus, mainly because the net goods and services balance improved while the net primary income balance remained negative.","A key reason was stronger export performance, especially in goods, supported by favourable commodity prices and demand for Australian resource exports. Although border closures during COVID-19 sharply reduced service exports such as tourism and education, those exports recovered as international travel resumed. This changed the composition of the current account over time, with services first weakening and then improving again after reopening.","Australia's savings-investment position also changed. Since 2017, domestic saving has been relatively strong compared with domestic investment, reducing the need for foreign borrowing. That contributed to changes in the financial account and the composition of capital flows compared with earlier periods when Australia relied more heavily on net capital inflow.","The persistent net primary income deficit reflects income paid to foreign owners of Australian assets, so even with stronger trade performance this part of the current account remained negative. Overall, movements in export earnings, service-trade disruptions and recovery, and changes in the saving-investment gap explain the recent trends in both the size and composition of Australia's Balance of Payments."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},vtt=["EC-H3"],ktt=["income inequality in Australia","gender and age dimensions of earnings inequality","economic effects of inequality on growth and wellbeing"],Ttt=["| Age | Male | Female |","|---|---:|---:|","| 15-19 years | AUD 600 | AUD 600 |","| 20-24 years | AUD 900 | AUD 850 |","| 25-34 years | AUD 1250 | AUD 1126.6 |","| 35-44 years | AUD 1500 | AUD 1200 |","| 45-54 years | AUD 1500 | AUD 1200 |","| 55-59 years | AUD 1500 | AUD 1117.3 |","| 60-64 years | AUD 1323.2 | AUD 1080 |","| 65 and over | AUD 1290.2 | AUD 1105.7 |"].join(`
`),Att={id:"economics/2025/q26",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...vtt],learningTopics:ktt,generate(t){return{question:["Explain how the dimensions and trends of income inequality affect the Australian economy. In your answer, refer to the information provided.","","Inequality is the uneven distribution of economic resources between individuals or different groups within a society. We care about inequality because income and wealth can be significant contributors to an individual's wellbeing and because inequality can be detrimental to the ongoing advancement of our economy and society.","","Median full-time weekly earnings by age and gender, 2024","",Ttt].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["Income inequality affects the Australian economy by changing spending patterns, access to opportunity and long-term growth. The information provided shows that inequality has dimensions linked to both age and gender, with female earnings below male earnings in most age groups and earnings also varying over the life cycle.","These income gaps can reduce access to quality housing, health care, education and technology for lower-income groups. Over time this weakens human capital formation and labour productivity, limiting the economy's productive potential. Lower-income households also tend to spend a larger share of their income on necessities, so inequality can restrain discretionary consumption and reduce aggregate demand growth.","There are also broader social and economic effects. Persistent inequality can reduce social cohesion, worsen health outcomes and make it harder for households to manage rising living costs. If people face barriers to participation or advancement, the economy may fail to use labour efficiently and lose output that could otherwise be produced.","At the same time, some income differences can create incentives to work, save and invest. However, when inequality becomes too wide or entrenched, the negative effects on wellbeing, opportunity and economic participation usually outweigh those benefits. This is why policies such as progressive taxation, transfer payments and improved access to services matter for both equity and economic performance in Australia."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},Stt=["EC-H4","EC-H3"],Ctt=["conflicts between economic objectives","trade-offs in macroeconomic policy","evaluating policy choices across competing goals"],Itt={id:"economics/2025/q27",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Stt],learningTopics:Ctt,generate(t){return{question:"Analyse the conflicts for government that may arise in pursuing Australia's economic objectives. In your response, refer to THREE separate objectives.",sampleAnswer:["Governments pursue a range of economic objectives, including economic growth, full employment, price stability, external stability, environmental sustainability and a more equitable distribution of income. These objectives are important, but they can conflict with each other, making policy decisions difficult.","One major conflict is between economic growth and price stability. Expansionary fiscal or monetary policy can increase aggregate demand, lift output and reduce unemployment, but if demand grows too strongly it can also increase inflation. This means policies that support growth and employment in the short run may make it harder to keep inflation within target.","A second conflict can arise between full employment and external stability. Strong domestic demand can reduce unemployment, but it may also increase imports and worsen the current account balance. Similarly, policies that slow demand to improve external stability may weaken growth and employment.","A third conflict exists between economic growth and environmental sustainability. Faster growth often increases production, transport and energy use, which can worsen pollution and resource depletion. Policies designed to protect the environment, such as stricter regulation or carbon pricing, may raise costs for firms in the short run and slow growth in some industries.","Governments therefore need to balance competing objectives rather than maximise one objective in isolation. The policy mix chosen will depend on current economic conditions and the relative importance of each objective at the time."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},_tt=["EC-H1","EC-H2"],qtt=["methods of protection","effects of protection on firms and government","efficiency and trade-offs of protectionist policies"],Ltt={id:"economics/2025/q28",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[..._tt],learningTopics:qtt,generate(t){return{question:"Analyse how different methods of protection affect firms and government in Australia.",sampleAnswer:["Protection refers to government policies that shelter domestic firms from foreign competition. Methods include tariffs, quotas, subsidies, local content rules and export incentives. These policies affect both firms and government in several ways.","For firms, protection can increase domestic market share and provide time to expand output, protect employment and build scale. Tariffs and quotas make imported goods more expensive or less available, so domestic firms can sell more easily in the local market. Subsidies can reduce production costs and improve competitiveness.","However, protection can also reduce pressure to become efficient. When firms face less foreign competition, they may have weaker incentives to cut costs, innovate or improve quality. Tariffs on imported inputs can also raise production costs for local firms that rely on overseas components.","For government, some protectionist policies provide revenue, especially tariffs. Protection can also help preserve employment in politically sensitive or strategic industries. But subsidies place pressure on the budget, and protectionist measures may create trade disputes, retaliation from trading partners and higher prices for consumers.","Overall, protection can support selected firms in the short term, but if it is maintained for too long it can reduce efficiency and create costs for both government and the broader economy."].join(`
`),marks:20}}},Ptt=[YZe,UZe,JZe,tet,oet,ret,det,pet,yet,wet,Tet,Iet,Pet,jet,Fet,Wet,Vet,Qet,ntt,att],Ett=[ctt,dtt,ftt,ytt,wtt,Att,Itt,Ltt],Ott={2015:{multipleChoicesGenerators:vHe,writingGenerators:kHe},2016:{multipleChoicesGenerators:eFe,writingGenerators:tFe},2017:{multipleChoicesGenerators:PDe,writingGenerators:EDe},2018:{multipleChoicesGenerators:hYe,writingGenerators:dYe},2019:{multipleChoicesGenerators:DWe,writingGenerators:zWe},2020:{multipleChoicesGenerators:xUe,writingGenerators:wUe},2021:{multipleChoicesGenerators:JVe,writingGenerators:ZVe},2022:{multipleChoicesGenerators:qKe,writingGenerators:LKe},2023:{multipleChoicesGenerators:cJe,writingGenerators:lJe},2024:{multipleChoicesGenerators:RZe,writingGenerators:FZe},2025:{multipleChoicesGenerators:Ptt,writingGenerators:Ett}};function Ntt(t,e,n){return[["Center Number:","","⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚","","Student Number:","","⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚ ⬚","","**HIGHER SCHOOL CERTIFICATE (HSC) EXAMINATION**"].join(`
