`),marks:3},{question:"Describe experimental evidence that supports time dilation.",sampleAnswer:["Muons are produced by cosmic rays colliding with the upper atmosphere","at altitudes of about 10–15 km. They travel toward Earth at speeds","of about $0.998c$.","","Muons have a half-life of about 2.2 μs in their own rest frame.","Classically, most muons should decay before reaching sea level","(classical prediction: very few detected at ground level).","","However, detectors at sea level measure **far more muons than","classical physics predicts**. This is because, from Earth's frame,","the muons' clocks run slow (time dilation extends their apparent","half-life), allowing them to survive the journey to ground level.","","The observed count at sea level agrees with the predictions of","special relativity, providing strong experimental support for","time dilation."].join(`
