`),sampleAnswer:["**Galvanometer $G_1$:**","","After the switch closes, a steady current flows through solenoid 1 from the battery.","Because this is a battery, the current is DC, so once the current has built up,","$G_1$ shows a **constant deflection** in one direction.","","**Galvanometer $G_2$:**","","As the switch closes, the current in solenoid 1 builds up from zero to its steady","value. This **changing** current produces a **changing magnetic flux** through","solenoid 2 (by Faraday's law), inducing an EMF in solenoid 2.","","$G_2$ shows a **brief deflection** (momentary kick) and then returns to zero","once the current in solenoid 1 has reached its steady value. At that stage the","magnetic field is no longer changing, so there is no induced EMF.","","**Direction of induced current (Lenz's law):**","","The induced current in solenoid 2 opposes the increasing flux from solenoid 1.","So the two facing ends become like poles and the solenoids **repel** each other.","","**Motion of the carts:**","","The repulsive magnetic force pushes the carts apart while the flux is changing.","By conservation of momentum (system initially at rest):","","$$p_1 + p_2 = 0 \\implies m_1 v_1 = m_2 v_2$$","","Since $m_1 = 2m_2$:","","$$v_1 = \\frac{v_2}{2}$$","","Cart 1 moves away in one direction **at half the speed** of cart 2, while cart 2","moves in the opposite direction faster because it has the smaller mass."].join(`
`),marks:7}}const fvn=1,gvn={id:"physics/2021/q31",version:1,variants:fvn,syllabusTopics:[...uvn],learningTopics:mvn,generate:pvn},$vn=["PH-M8-1"],yvn=["Hubble expansion models","elastic band analogy","limitations of cosmological models"];function bvn(t){return{question:["A model of Hubble expansion uses an elastic band with evenly spaced markings.","One end of the elastic band is fixed. When the elastic band is stretched,","each spacing doubles.","","Evaluate this as a model for Hubble's law. Discuss both similarities to","and limitations of the model."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Similarities to Hubble's law:**","","- Markings further from the fixed end move a greater distance when the","  elastic is stretched — markings at distance $d$ move by $d$ (double),",'  while markings at $2d$ move by $2d$. Recession "speed" is proportional',"  to distance from the fixed end, matching **Hubble's law**: $v \\propto d$.","",'- The model shows that no single marking is the "centre" of expansion',"  (except for the artificial fixed end), illustrating that distant markings","  recede faster.","","**Limitations of the model:**","","- The elastic band has a **fixed end** (an edge/centre), whereas the real","  universe has no centre or boundary — every galaxy sees all others receding.","","- The model is **one-dimensional**, whereas the universe expands in three dimensions.","","- The **markings themselves stretch** as the elastic stretches, but galaxies","  themselves do not expand (they are held together by gravity).","","- The model represents only a single instant of stretching, not a continuous","  expansion over time at a measurable recession velocity (Hubble measured","  redshift, not physical stretching of objects)."].join(`
