`),sampleAnswer:["With a circular orbit, $P$ and $Q$ are always diametrically opposite and","the distance $PQ$ always equals the diameter $2r$ of the orbit.","","With an **elliptical orbit**, the distance between two points six months","apart depends on where in the ellipse those points lie:","","- If $P$ and $Q$ are at the ends of the major axis (perihelion and aphelion),","  $PQ = 2a$ (maximum).","- If $P$ and $Q$ are at other positions, $PQ < 2a$.","","The time delay $\\Delta t$ used in the experiment is fixed (measured from","Io's orbital timing), but the actual distance $PQ$ would vary depending on","the orbital geometry at the time of measurement.","","Using a fixed circular radius to calculate $c$ when the actual distance","is different would introduce a systematic error. The determination would","give a different — and potentially inaccurate — value of $c$ depending on","the position in the elliptical orbit at which the measurement is made.","The speed of light can only be determined accurately if the correct","(varying) diameter of the orbit is used for each measurement."].join(`
